{"kind":"report","text":"Publication-route recommendation for the I7 stage-1 metered evaluator, fulfilling promise fc6503ba on request #4211. Full report attached as report.md with the raw repository facts (repo-facts.txt, upstream-facts.txt); all load-bearing facts were re-checked directly by checker. Confirmed: the patch at e9fa318e is 78 commits and 133 files (+19380/-18) over upstream base 599f35f3, every path under v206/, with go.mod, go.sum and LICENSE unchanged and no replace introduced (the only replace is upstream's own in the separate exerciser module); v206 has no go.mod, so it is an ordinary import subpath of a v0 module and carries no Go major-version rule; five shared-core callers, all importing only the v206 subpath: internal/projection/projection_test.go in the root module and jsonataddl/application.go, compile.go, confine.go and evaluate.go in the jsonataddl module; both go.mod files pin github.com/jsonata-go/jsonata v0.0.0-20250709164031-599f35f32e5f. Upstream jsonata-go/jsonata is Blues-owned, public, MIT, not archived, last pushed 2025-07-09, with zero pull requests, zero issues and zero forks ever, no tags or releases, an unobservable maintainer roster (collaborators endpoint 403) and a fork-and-PR contributing policy; checker holds pull access only. Destination generalbusiness-ai/jsonata does not exist (404); the organization already publishes Go modules from GitHub (tailapps/jsonataddl on the proxy). Owner-dependent: whether upstream would ever accept the patch, who the maintainers are, whether the organization wants to own a fork, who creates the repository, visibility and pin form. Route A (upstream acceptance) keeps the module path unchanged but depends entirely on a silent third party with no precedent and no timescale, so it is not supportable as the stage-1 pinning route; it stays an optional later contribution. Recommendation: Route B, a public organization-maintained fork github.com/generalbusiness-ai/jsonata, forked by pushing upstrea